1	# Observer
2	
3	Let an object announce that something happened, and let any number of listeners react, without the announcing object
4	knowing who they are.
5	
6	## Use it when
7	
8	- one event must trigger several independent reactions (update a display, raise an alarm, write a log);
9	- new reactions to the same event are expected, and today each one would mean editing the code that raises it;
10	- the reactions belong to other parts of the system, and the source should not depend on them.
11	
12	## Do not use it when
13	
14	- there is exactly one reaction and nothing suggests more: a direct call is clearer;
15	- the reactions must happen in a strict order with results that feed each other: that is a sequence of calls, or a
16	  pipeline;
17	- the "listener list" would always contain the same single object.
18	
19	## Shape
20	
21	```java
22	interface ReadingListener {
23	    void onReading(SensorReading reading);
24	}
25	
26	final class WeatherStation {
27	    private final List<ReadingListener> listeners;           // received from outside
28	    WeatherStation(List<ReadingListener> listeners) { this.listeners = List.copyOf(listeners); }
29	
30	    void record(SensorReading reading) {
31	        // … store the reading …
32	        for (ReadingListener listener : listeners) listener.onReading(reading);
33	    }
34	}
35	
36	final class Display implements ReadingListener { /* show it */ }
37	final class FrostAlarm implements ReadingListener { /* only reacts below zero: the filter is its own */ }
38	final class History implements ReadingListener { /* keep it */ }
39	
40	// the wiring of the application decides who listens
41	WeatherStation station = new WeatherStation(List.of(new Display(), new FrostAlarm(), new History()));
42	```
43	
44	## Done right when
45	
46	- the source only announces the event; it builds no reaction and names no listener;
47	- each reaction is its own listener, with its own rules about when to act;
48	- the reactions that existed before are listeners too, not direct calls next to the new ones;
49	- the list of listeners is assembled outside the source, where the application is wired;
50	- adding a reaction means one new listener and one line where they are registered.
51	
52	## Usual mistakes
53	
54	- One central class that receives the event and performs every reaction itself: the code moved, the coupling did
55	  not.
56	- The source still builds the content of some reactions and only delegates the others.
57	- The listener list is created inside the class that dispatches the events, so adding a listener still means editing
58	  it.
59	- Only some events go through listeners, and others are still handled by direct calls.
60	
61	## Neighbors
62	
63	- **Mediator**: coordinates objects that talk to each other in both directions; Observer is one-way.
64	- **Command**: when the reaction must be queued, retried or undone.
65

